=== layout: multiple-choice

@question

![A to B to C; enzyme A catalyzes the first step and enzyme B the second](assets/lect02b-genes_dogma/metabolic_abc.drawn.svg)

The figure above shows a simple metabolic pathway. According to Beadle and Tatum's hypothesis, how many genes are necessary for this pathway?

- A. 1
- B. 2
- C. 3
- D. It cannot be determined from the pathway.

@answer

Answer\: B

=== layout: multiple-choice

@question

![A to B to C; enzyme A catalyzes the first step and enzyme B the second](assets/lect02b-genes_dogma/metabolic_abc.drawn.svg)

Product C is required for growth.

- Strains mutant for the gene-encoding enzyme A would be able to grow on medium supplemented with \_\_\_\_\_.
  - A. nutrient A only
  - B. nutrient B only
  - C. nutrient C only
  - D. nutrients B or C
  - E. nutrients A or C

@answer

Answer\: D

=== layout: multiple-choice

@question

![A to B to C; enzyme A catalyzes the first step and enzyme B the second](assets/lect02b-genes_dogma/metabolic_abc.drawn.svg)

Product C is required for growth.

- Strains mutant for the gene-encoding enzyme B would be able to grow on medium supplemented with \_\_\_\_\_.
  - A. nutrient A only
  - B. nutrient B only
  - C. nutrient C only
  - D. nutrients B or C
  - E. nutrients A or C

@answer

Answer\: C

=== layout: multiple-choice

@question

- Enzyme A

- Enzyme B

- Enzyme C

- A  ->  B  ->  C  ->  D

Molecule D is the only molecule required for growth.

- Strains mutant for the gene-encoding enzyme B would be able to grow on medium supplemented with \_\_\_\_\_.
  - A) nutrient D only
  - B) nutrients A and D
  - C) nutrients B and D
  - D) nutrients C and D
  - E) nutrients B, C, and D
  - F) nutrients A, B, and D
  - G) nutrients A, C, and D

@answer

Answer\: D

=== layout: one-panel

# Gene = Polypeptide

@body

- Many proteins are composed of several polypeptides, each of which has its own gene. Beadle and Tatum's hypothesis is now\:
  - one gene-one polypeptide
- Note that it is common to refer to gene products as proteins rather than polypeptides

=== layout: multiple-choice

@question

Which of the following contradicts the one-gene, one-enzyme hypothesis?

- A) A mutation in a single gene can result in a defective protein.
- B) Alkaptonuria results when individuals lack a single enzyme involved in the catalysis of homogentisic acid.
- C) Sickle-cell anemia results in defective hemoglobin.
- D) A single antibody gene can code for different related proteins, depending on the splicing that takes place post-transcriptionally.

@answer

Answer\: D

=== layout: section

# The Genetic Code From RNA to Protein

=== layout: big-image

@image

![DNA template, mRNA codons, and Trp-Phe-Gly-Ser protein](assets/lect02b-genes_dogma/dna_rna_protein.drawn.svg)

@caption

DNA molecule: genes 1, 2, and 3. Transcription uses the DNA template strand; translation reads mRNA codons to produce amino acids in a protein.

=== layout: one-panel

# The genetic code

@body

![Slide image 11](assets/lect02b-genes_dogma/image_011.jpg)

=== layout: multiple-choice

@question

What does it mean when we say the genetic code is redundant?

- A) A single codon (three nucleotides) can specify the addition of more than one amino acid.
- B) The genetic code is different for different domains of organisms.
- C) The genetic code is universal (the same for all organisms).
- D) More than one codon can specify the addition of the same amino acid.

@answer

Answer\: D

=== layout: stacked-panels

# Genetic Code

@top

- The genetic code is redundant and unambiguous
  - Redundant\:
    - degenerate; being in excess; characterized by verbosity or unnecessary repetition
    - two different codons can specify the same amino acid
  - Unambiguous\:
    - unequivocal; clear; indistinct; having only a single meaning or interpretation
    - no codon specifies more than one amino acid

@bottom

- codon

- codon

- codon

- amino acid




- amino acid



=== layout: section

# Mutation

=== layout: big-image

@image

![The M1V mutant in the PAH gene, codons 1 through 7](assets/lect02b-genes_dogma/pah_m1v.drawn.svg)

@caption

Mutations can lead to altered proteins. The M1V mutant in the PAH gene: mutation of A/T to G/C at codon 1 changes the initiation codon. No PAH polypeptide is produced because tRNA^Val^ cannot be used to initiate polypeptide synthesis.

=== layout: big-image

@image

![The R408W mutant in the PAH gene, with DNA, mRNA, tRNA anticodons and polypeptide](assets/lect02b-genes_dogma/pah_r408w.drawn.svg)

@caption

The R408W mutant in the PAH gene: mutation of C/G to T/A at codon 408. Mutant codon in PAH mRNA; mutant amino acid in PAH polypeptide.

=== layout: big-image

@image

![Template DNA CTT versus CAT gives mRNA GAA versus GUA and Glu versus Val](assets/lect02b-genes_dogma/hemoglobin_point_mutation.drawn.svg)

@caption

The molecular basis of sickle-cell disease: a point mutation.

=== layout: two-panels


@left

![Schematic normal and clumped hemoglobin tetramers](assets/lect02b-genes_dogma/hemoglobin_assembly.drawn.svg)

@right

![Retained molecular surface with mutation callout](assets/lect02b-genes_dogma/image_016.png)

=== layout: one-panel


@body

![Slide image 17](assets/lect02b-genes_dogma/image_017.jpg)

=== layout: big-image

@image

![DNA-to-product analogy: normal](assets/lect02b-genes_dogma/analogy_normal.drawn.svg)

@caption

Suppose there is a strand of DNA that codes for a chair-shaped protein.

=== layout: big-image

@image

![DNA-to-product analogy: silent](assets/lect02b-genes_dogma/analogy_silent.drawn.svg)

@caption

If that DNA got a "silent" mutation, it would not change the protein at all.

=== layout: big-image

@image

![DNA-to-product analogy: missense](assets/lect02b-genes_dogma/analogy_missense.drawn.svg)

@caption

However, if a "missense" mutation occurs, a single portion of the protein's structure would change (which can massively alter the function).

=== layout: big-image

@image

![DNA-to-product analogy: nonsense](assets/lect02b-genes_dogma/analogy_nonsense.drawn.svg)

@caption

In the case of a "nonsense" (or chain-termination) mutation, the translation into a chair would be stopped early, leading to an incomplete structure.

=== layout: big-image

@image

![DNA-to-product analogy: frameshift](assets/lect02b-genes_dogma/analogy_frameshift.drawn.svg)

@caption

But a "frameshift" mutation turns the rest of the information into a completely different sequence, resulting in the creation of an entirely different protein.

